# Line endings, pinned — because two committed files in this repository are compared to a freshly
# generated one byte for byte: `packages/dsh-app/cordis.patch.yml`, which `bundle.test.ts`
# re-renders, and `packages/dsh-app/dump-config.golden.yml`, which `golden.test.ts` re-captures.
#
# Git's Windows default is `core.autocrlf=true`, which stores LF and checks out CRLF. Both files are
# LF in the index, so without this line a fresh clone on Windows would hand those tests CRLF and
# fail them — while CI, on Linux, stayed green. That failure mode is worth closing at the source
# rather than by teaching each test to normalise: a test that ignores line endings also ignores a
# generator that started emitting the wrong ones.
#
# `text=auto` still lets Git detect and leave binary files alone; there are none today, and this
# keeps that from mattering later. Nothing here needs CRLF: the repository has no `.bat` or `.cmd`.
* text=auto eol=lf
